    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, additive clone of the established create-time-check pattern:\n\n- **Right unit, consistent shape.** `CheckDailyLimit` mirrors `CheckCityCapacity` exactly \u2014 same `service(...)` signature, same `findAll()` counting loop, same `static final` limit constant, same \"runs among the create-time checks, before `BuildOwner`\" Javadoc framing. A maintainer would recognize it immediately.\n- **Exception and handler match the package conventions.** `DailyLimitExceededException` follows `CityAtCapacityException` (checked, carries the limit, same Javadoc structure); `DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler` uses the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper \"like every other handler in this package,\" matching `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`. The one difference \u2014 not setting a field-level `errors` property \u2014 is correct here, since a daily cap is not tied to a request field.\n- **Wiring is idiomatic.** The YAML step is inserted into the existing `next:` chain between `checkCityCapacity` and `build`, with `govern: [ transaction ]` like every sibling step, and the escalation YAML follows the `<FQCN>.yml` convention.\n- **No duplication or catch-all growth.** The `findAll()` loop is the house style for these checks rather than a new abstraction worth introducing for one call site; no existing class is being overloaded with unrelated responsibilities.\n\n(Per the task I did not judge correctness/efficiency, e.g. the `findAll()`-then-filter approach \u2014 that matches the existing check and is a correctness/perf question, not a structural one.)",
